Hi,
I'm wondering if someone can tell me what exactly this program is doing when you use the double sampling approach to estimate occupancy. I am having trouble finding an explanation in the literature of exactly how the modeling is performed to estimate occupancy using a double sampling approach. I am working with the same dataset as described above, so that I have 10 islands resurveyed for detection/non-detection data and 190 islands that were surveyed only once.
It appears to me that the program is using data from islands that were resurveyed to estimate the detection probability using the maximum likelihood approach of MacKenzie et al. (2002 Ecology). Then the program uses this detection probability to estimate the occupancy on the remaining 190 islands following the same maximum likelihood approach of MacKenzie et al. (2002 Ecology). Or is the program doing something else?
I'd appreciate any help that you folks can provide.
Thank you,
Mikey

On Wednesday, August 28, 2013 6:48:00 AM UTC-6, Richard Chandler wrote:
> Hi Mikey,
>
> This should work. Can you send a reproducible example that shows the problem?
>
> Richard
>
>
> On Tue, Aug 27, 2013 at 2:30 PM, Mikey T <tab...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > Hi,
> > I used double sampling to estimate detection probability (note this is distinct from the double observer sampling appoarch; the method is described in MacKenzie and Royle 2005 J App Ecol). This means that I visited 190 sites once, and 10 sites 5 times. I realize this is not ideal, but I'd like to model occupancy using these data.
> > Is there a way to use unmarked to run occu using these data? I am running into a problem because for 190 rows in my dataframe, there are NA's for 4 of the 5 columns. I assume this is why I get the message: "dims [product 200] do not match the length of object [1000]."
> > When I run occu for just the 10 rows where I sampled each site evenly it is not problem and the model works fine, but obviously I'd like to run the model using data from all sites.
> > Has anyone tried to use unmarked for data where they estimated detection probability using a Double Sampling approach? Is there a way to run the model and input a specific detection probability instead of having the program calculate it for you? Does anyone have any suggestions?
> > Thank you,
> > Mikey
